**Short answer**

*You can start with a plain GeoJSON source for the 8 000 trail segments and a hosted vector‑basemap (plus an optional `raster‑dem` for terrain).*  
If the GeoJSON file stays under **≈ 5 MB** after you simplify the line geometries, the browser will load and parse it in a couple of seconds and the click‑to‑show‑name/distance interaction will work perfectly.  

If the file grows larger than **≈ 5 – 10 MB** (or you notice a noticeable lag on mobile), switch the trails to a tiny vector‑tile source generated with **tippecanoe** (or **Planetiler**) and keep the basemap as a separate vector source.  

Below is a step‑by‑step guide for both paths, plus the “when‑to‑upgrade” checklist.

---

## 1️⃣ What you need in the final style

| Layer | Source type | Why |
|-------|-------------|-----|
| **Basemap (roads, labels, land‑cover)** | `vector` (hosted style URL or TileJSON) | Gives you crisp, zoom‑dependent rendering and built‑in label placement. |
| **Terrain (hillshade / 3‑D terrain)** | `raster-dem` (optional) | Provides elevation for hillshading or MapLibre’s terrain API. |
| **Hiking trails** | **Either** `geojson` **or** `vector` (tiny tiles) | Clickable features with name & distance. |
| **Optional UI layers** (e.g., highlighted trail on hover) | Same source as trails, separate style layers. | Keeps interaction fast. |

All three sources can coexist in a single style; MapLibre will request them in parallel and composite the layers in draw order.

---

## 2️⃣ Option A – Use GeoJSON (the simplest, and usually enough for ~8 k line features)

### 2.1 Prepare the GeoJSON

1. **Simplify the line geometry** – you don’t need sub‑meter precision for a map that will be viewed at 1 – 16 × zoom.  
   *Tools*: `mapshaper -simplify 5%`, `ogr2ogr -simplify`, or the `simplify-geojson` npm package.  
2. **Round coordinates** to 5‑6 decimal places (≈ 0.1 m) – reduces file size without visual loss.  
3. **Strip any unnecessary properties** – keep only `name`, `distance`, and maybe an `id`.  
4. **Compress** – serve the file with gzip/ Brotli (most CDNs do this automatically).  

> **Target size:** ≤ 5 MB (≈ 0.6 KB per feature). 8 000 line features usually end up well under this after simplification.

### 2.2 Add the source & layers

```javascript
// 1️⃣ Basemap – use a hosted vector style (e.g. MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, or your own TileJSON)
map.addSource('basemap', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets-v2/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY'
});

// 2️⃣ Terrain (optional)
map.addSource('terrain', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-v2/tiles.json?key=YOUR_KEY',
  tileSize: 512,
  maxzoom: 14
});
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });

// 3️⃣ Trails – GeoJSON source
map.addSource('trails', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: '/data/hiking-trails.geojson'   // or an inline object
});

// 4️⃣ Trail line layer (drawn *under* the basemap’s label layers)
map.addLayer({
  id: 'trails-line',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'trails',
  layout: { 'line-cap': 'round', 'line-join': 'round' },
  paint: {
    'line-color': '#ff6600',
    'line-width': [
      'interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
      10, 2,
      14, 4,
      18, 8
    ]
  }
}, firstSymbolLayerId);   // insert before the first symbol layer so labels stay on top

// 5️⃣ Optional highlight layer for hover / click
map.addLayer({
  id: 'trails-highlight',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'trails',
  filter: ['==', 'id', ''],   // empty filter – will be set on hover
  paint: {
    'line-color': '#ffff00',
    'line-width': 6
  }
});
```

### 2.3 Click interaction (show name & distance)

```javascript
map.on('click', 'trails-line', (e) => {
  const feature = e.features[0];
  const name = feature.properties.name || 'Unnamed trail';
  const dist = feature.properties.distance
               ? `${feature.properties.distance} km`
               : '—';
  new maplibregl.Popup()
    .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
    .setHTML(`<strong>${name}</strong><br/>${dist}`)
    .addTo(map);
});
```

### 2.4 When this setup *fails* (performance checklist)

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **> 3 s pause before the map appears** | GeoJSON > 5 MB, many vertices per line | Simplify further, or switch to vector tiles (see §3). |
| **Mobile browsers freeze / “out of memory”** | Too many vertices, no simplification | Reduce vertex count, use `tippecanoe` to generate tiles. |
| **Interaction lag when hovering** | Large feature collection being queried each frame | Add a `filter`‑based highlight layer, or use vector tiles (faster spatial index). |

If any of the above happen, move to **Option B**.

---

## 3️⃣ Option B – Convert the trails to a tiny vector‑tile source

### 3.1 Why you’d do this

* **File‑size advantage** – a 8 000‑line GeoJSON (≈ 5 MB) can become a ~300 KB MBTiles/PMTiles set (≈ 30 KB per zoom level).  
* **Viewport‑only loading** – only the tiles covering the current view are fetched, so even a large region stays fast.  
* **Built‑in spatial index** – `queryRenderedFeatures` works on the client without scanning the whole dataset.

### 3.2 Build the tiles (one‑time step)

```bash
# 1️⃣ Install tippecanoe (binary)
# 2️⃣ Generate a MBTiles file (zoom 5‑14 is plenty for trails)
tippecanoe \
  --output=hiking-trails.mbtiles \
  --layer=trails \
  --minimum-zoom=5 \
  --maximum-zoom=14 \
  --drop-densest-as-needed \
  --simplify-only-low-zoom \
  --force \
  hiking-trails.geojson
```

*If you prefer a modern, cloud‑native format, run `mbutil` or `pmtiles convert` to get a **PMTiles** archive, which can be served directly from S3, Cloudflare R2, or any static‑file host.*

### 3.3 Serve the tiles

| Hosting option | How to do it |
|----------------|--------------|
| **Static file host (S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages)** | Upload the `.mbtiles` (or `.pmtiles`) and use a tiny serverless function (e.g., Cloudflare Workers, Netlify Functions) that reads the archive and returns `application/x-protobuf` tiles. The **maplibre‑pmtiles‑patterns** skill shows a ready‑to‑use worker. |
| **Tile server (TileServer‑GL, Tileserver‑PHP, t-rex)** | Point it at the MBTiles file and expose `/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`. |
| **Hosted service (MapTiler Cloud, Planetiler‑as‑a‑service)** | Upload the MBTiles and get a TileJSON URL. |

The result is a **TileJSON** URL like:

```
https://mytiles.example.com/trails/tiles.json
```

### 3.4 Wire the vector source in MapLibre

```javascript
// 1️⃣ Basemap (same as before)
map.addSource('basemap', { type: 'vector', url: basemapStyleUrl });

// 2️⃣ Terrain (optional)
map.addSource('terrain', { type: 'raster-dem', url: terrainTileJSON });

// 3️⃣ Trails – vector source
map.addSource('trails', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'https://mytiles.example.com/trails/tiles.json'   // TileJSON URL
});

// 4️⃣ Trail line layer (source-layer name comes from the MBTiles layer)
map.addLayer({
  id: 'trails-line',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'trails',
  'source-layer': 'trails',   // same as the `--layer` name used in tippecanoe
  layout: { 'line-cap': 'round', 'line-join': 'round' },
  paint: {
    'line-color': '#ff6600',
    'line-width': [
      'interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
      10, 2,
      14, 4,
      18, 8
    ]
  }
}, firstSymbolLayerId);
```

The click handler is identical; MapLibre will now query only the tiles that intersect the click point, which is *much* faster on low‑end devices.

### 3.5 When to stop using vector tiles

| Situation | Reason to stay with GeoJSON |
|-----------|----------------------------|
| The trail dataset is **static** and you can afford a one‑off tile build. | Vector tiles are still fine; the extra step is only for performance. |
| You need **client‑side editing** of the trails (draw, modify, re‑save). | GeoJSON is easier; you can still overlay a vector basemap. |
| The total size after tiling is **< 2 MB** and you have no mobile‑only audience. | GeoJSON is simpler and avoids a tile server. |

---

