Below is a step‑by‑step recipe for turning a single scanned JPG (or any georeferenced raster image) into a **MapLibre `image` source**, adding it as a raster layer, and exposing a simple opacity toggle.  
The pattern works for any one‑off historic map, a scanned city plan, a PDF‑to‑JPG export, etc.

---

## 1. Why an `image` source?

| Source type | When it shines | What it does for you |
|-------------|----------------|----------------------|
| **`image`** | One static raster (JPG/PNG/WebP) with known corner coordinates | No tile server needed; MapLibre fetches the single file, warps it to the supplied four corner points, and draws it as a raster layer. |
| **`raster` tiles** | Large imagery that must be viewable at many zoom levels (satellite, historic map series) | You would pre‑generate a tile pyramid (e.g., with `gdal2tiles`, MapTiler, or PMTiles). Overkill for a single map sheet. |
| **`geojson`** | Vector data, not raster images | Not applicable here. |

So the **`image` source** is the “right” choice for a single scanned map.

---

## 2. Prepare the image file

1. **Host the JPG** where the browser can fetch it (your own web server, a CDN, or a static‑file bucket).  
   *Make sure CORS is allowed* (`Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` or your domain) – otherwise the browser will block the request.

2. **Know the four corner coordinates** in **[lon, lat]** order (MapLibre expects WGS‑84).  
   Example (clockwise from top‑left):

   ```text
   top‑left     = [ -122.452, 37.804 ]   // lon, lat
   top‑right    = [ -122.438, 37.804 ]
   bottom‑right = [ -122.438, 37.791 ]
   bottom‑left  = [ -122.452, 37.791 ]
   ```

   Put them in an array in that exact order.

---

## 3. Add the source & layer in JavaScript

```javascript
// 1️⃣  Create the MapLibre map (skip if you already have one)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
  center: [-122.445, 37.797],     // initial view
  zoom: 13
});

// 2️⃣  When the style is ready, add the historic image source
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // ---- IMAGE SOURCE -------------------------------------------------
  map.addSource('historic-map', {
    type: 'image',
    url: 'https://example.com/historic-1920s-city.jpg', // <-- your JPG URL
    coordinates: [
      [-122.452, 37.804], // top‑left
      [-122.438, 37.804], // top‑right
      [-122.438, 37.791], // bottom‑right
      [-122.452, 37.791]  // bottom‑left
    ]
  });

  // ---- RASTER LAYER -------------------------------------------------
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'historic-map-layer',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'historic-map',
    paint: {
      // Start fully opaque; we’ll toggle this later
      'raster-opacity': 0.8
    }
  });

  // ---- OPTIONAL: Insert below label layers -------------------------
  // If you want city‑name / road labels to stay on top:
  const labelLayers = map.getStyle().layers.filter(l => l.type === 'symbol');
  const firstLabelId = labelLayers.length ? labelLayers[0].id : null;
  if (firstLabelId) {
    map.moveLayer('historic-map-layer', firstLabelId);
  }
});
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| `type: 'image'` | Declares a single‑image source. |
| `url` | Direct link to the JPG. |
| `coordinates` | Four corner points (lon‑lat) that tell MapLibre how to warp the image onto the globe. |
| `type: 'raster'` | Renders the image as a raster layer (the only layer type that can consume an `image` source). |
| `raster-opacity` | Controls transparency; we’ll change it later. |
| `moveLayer` (optional) | Places the historic map **under** any symbol (label) layers so street names stay readable. |

---

## 4. UI for toggling opacity

You can expose a simple HTML range slider, a button, or any UI framework you like. Below is a minimal example using a range input (0 – 100 % opacity).

```html
<div id="opacity-control" style="position: absolute; top: 10px; left: 10px; background: white; padding: 5px; z-index: 1;">
  <label for="opacity-slider">Historic map opacity:</label>
  <input id="opacity-slider" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="80">
</div>
```

```javascript
// Hook the slider to the raster-opacity paint property
const slider = document.getElementById('opacity-slider');
slider.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
  const opacity = e.target.value / 100; // convert 0‑100 → 0‑1
  map.setPaintProperty('historic-map-layer', 'raster-opacity', opacity);
});
```

**Result:** Users can slide from fully transparent (0 %) to fully opaque (100 %). The change is instantaneous because the image is already in memory.

---

## 5. (Optional) If you need more zoom levels

A single JPG will look pixelated when you zoom far in. If you anticipate users zooming beyond the native resolution of the scan, consider **pre‑tiling** the image:

| Tool | What it does | Output you can use |
|------|--------------|--------------------|
| **`gdal2tiles.py`** (GDAL) | Generates a classic XYZ tile pyramid (PNG/WEBP) from any raster. | Serve the tiles via a static web server or a CDN; add them as a `raster` source (`type: 'raster', tiles: ['https://…/{z}/{x}/{y}.png']`). |
| **MapTiler** (desktop/web) | Same as GDAL but with UI, optional compression, and MBTiles/PMTiles export. | MBTiles/PMTiles can be served with the **PMTiles** plugin (see `maplibre-pmtiles-patterns` skill). |
| **PMTiles** | Packs the whole tile pyramid into a single file that can be served via a CDN edge function. | Add a `raster` source with `url: 'pmtiles://https://…/historic.pmtiles'`. |

For a *single* historic sheet, the extra work is only worth it if you need crisp detail at high zoom (e.g., > 1:5 000). Otherwise the simple `image` source is perfectly fine.

---

## 6. Full example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Historic map overlay</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    #opacity-control { font-family: sans-serif; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<div id="opacity-control">
  <label for="opacity-slider">Historic map opacity:</label>
  <input id="opacity-slider" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="80">
</div>

<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.445, 37.797],
    zoom: 13
  });

  map.on('style.load', () => {
    map.addSource('historic-map', {
      type: 'image',
      url: 'https://example.com/historic-1920s-city.jpg',
      coordinates: [
        [-122.452, 37.804],
        [-122.438, 37.804],
        [-122.438, 37.791],
        [-122.452, 37.791]
      ]
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'historic-map-layer',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'historic-map',
      paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.8 }
    });

    // Put the historic map under any label layers
    const labelLayers = map.getStyle().layers.filter(l => l.type === 'symbol');
    if (labelLayers.length) {
      map.moveLayer('historic-map-layer', labelLayers[0].id);
    }
  });

  // Opacity UI
  const slider = document.getElementById('opacity-slider');
  slider.addEventListener('input', e => {
    const opacity = e.target.value / 100;
    map.setPaintProperty('historic-map-layer', 'raster-opacity', opacity);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Copy‑paste, replace the `url` and `coordinates` with your own values, and you’re done.

---

## 7. Checklist & Gotchas

| ✅ | Item |
|----|------|
| **CORS** | The JPG must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` that includes your page’s origin (or `*`). |
| **Coordinate order** | `coordinates` is **[lon, lat]**, not `[lat, lon]`. |
| **Projection** | MapLibre works in Web Mercator (EPSG:3857). The corner points must be in WGS‑84 (lon/lat). |
| **Image size** | Very large JPGs (tens of MB) increase initial load time. If the file is > 5 MB, consider compressing it (WebP) or tiling it. |
| **Opacity range** | `raster-opacity` expects a value between `0` (transparent) and `1` (opaque). |
| **Layer order** | If you want the historic map *behind* labels, move the layer before the first `symbol` layer (see code above). |
| **Mobile** | The same code works on mobile browsers; just be mindful of data usage if the image is large. |

---

## 8. When to move beyond `image`

| Situation | Recommended upgrade |
|-----------|----------------------|
| Users need to zoom past the native resolution of the scan (pixelation) | Generate raster tiles (`gdal2tiles`, MapTiler, or PMTiles) and use a `raster` source. |
| You have many historic sheets that you want to serve as a seamless mosaic | Create a tiled raster dataset (or a vector‑based georeferenced PDF → raster tiles) and serve via a tile server. |
| You want to query individual features (e.g., parcels) inside the historic map | Digitize the map into vector data and serve as a `vector` source (or `geojson` if small). |

For a **single scanned map**, the `image` source is the simplest, most performant, and fully supported way.

---

### TL;DR

1. Host the JPG with CORS enabled.  
2. Add an `image` source with the four corner `[lon, lat]` points.  
3. Add a `raster` layer that uses that source and set an initial `raster-opacity`.  
4. (Optional) Move the layer under label layers.  
5. Hook a UI control to `map.setPaintProperty(..., 'raster-opacity', value)`.

That’s all you need to overlay a historic 1920s city map on a MapLibre GL JS map and let users fade it in/out. Happy mapping!
